= Guaco

_Status: a Brazilian / South-American native_ Asteraceae _climbing vine of the_ Mikania _genus — principally_ Mikania glomerata _Spreng. (the species canonical in the Brazilian Pharmacopoeia) and the closely-related_ Mikania laevigata _Sch.Bip. ex Baker (with substantial commercial-supply species substitution and the modern Brazilian Pharmacopoeia distinguishing the two). Foundational to Brazilian folk medicine as the_ canonical Brazilian respiratory and antitussive herb — _the substance most cited in Brazilian ethnobotanical surveys for cough, bronchitis, and asthma; second only to honey in the Brazilian cough-syrup tradition; and the single Brazilian native medicinal with the most-developed_ industrial-commercial pharmaceutical register — _the_ xarope de guaco _(guaco syrup) being available in nearly every Brazilian pharmacy and supermarket as a foundational over-the-counter respiratory product. The substance is exclusively South-American and post-Columbian to all four canonical Eurasian medical traditions; the substantive tradition is_ Brazilian-and-South-American folk-medical, _with a uniquely successful_ folk-to-industrial-pharmacy transition _that distinguishes guaco from most Brazilian native medicinals. Active principle:_ coumarin _(the principal modern marker, with documented antitussive and expectorant activity)._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Mikania glomerata_ Spreng., family Asteraceae (Compositae), tribe Eupatorieae. Older synonyms include _Cacalia glomerata_ Spreng., _Eupatorium glomeratum_ Spreng. The drug is principally the *leaves* (Mikaniae glomeratae folium in modern pharmacognosy) — the canonical commercial-and-pharmaceutical drug-form; some traditional preparations use the *flowering stems* and *aerial parts*. The closely-related Brazilian / South-American _Mikania_ species variously called guaco:
- *_Mikania laevigata_ Sch.Bip. ex Baker* — the alternative canonical guaco species; very closely related to _M. glomerata_; substantial commercial supply substitution; the modern Brazilian Pharmacopoeia distinguishes the two but accepts both as guaco. _M. laevigata_ has somewhat higher coumarin content in many surveys.
- *_Mikania cordifolia_ (L.f.) Willd.* — North-and-Central American species; also called guaco; foundational Mexican folk medicine.
- *_Mikania guaco_ Bonpl.* — the original Humboldt-Bonpland-described species; foundational to the genus naming.
- *_Mikania micrantha_ Kunth* — pantropical invasive; also called guaco in some Asian-introduced regions.

The genus *_Mikania_* (~430 species globally) commemorates *Joseph Gottfried Mikan* (1743-1814), Bohemian-Austrian botanist. The genus is one of the larger genera of Asteraceae and contains substantial pantropical climbing vines with extensive folk-medical use across the Americas, Asia, and Africa.

The species epithet *_glomerata_* = "clustered, gathered into a head" — referring to the densely-clustered small white flower-heads characteristic of _M. glomerata_.#footnote[Species identity and synonymy: _Flora do Brasil_ (Jardim Botânico do Rio de Janeiro); Robinson and Holmes _Mikania revisions_; Brazilian Pharmacopoeia (Farmacopeia Brasileira) monographs on _M. glomerata_ and _M. laevigata_. The species substitution and commercial-supply considerations are documented in modern Brazilian phytopharmacy literature.]

*English:* *Guaco* (the Portuguese / Spanish vernacular adopted directly into English specialty herbal-medicine literature); *climbing hempweed* (the broader genus-vernacular in English); *bittervine* (for invasive _M. micrantha_).

*Portuguese (Brazilian):* *guaco* — the canonical name; Spanish-and-Portuguese cognate with substantial South-American currency. The etymology is *indigenous* — from the *Cumanagoto* (a Carib-language people of northeastern Venezuela) or the related *Chibcha-language* family; the term *guaco* in the indigenous source-language referred broadly to *snake-bite-curing plants* — reflecting the pre-Columbian use of various Mikania species for snake-and-insect-bite. The Portuguese-Spanish adoption preserves the indigenous-cure register etymologically. Regional Brazilian variants and related vernaculars:
- *Guaco* (general Brazilian) — _M. glomerata_, sometimes _M. laevigata_.
- *Guaco-cheiroso* ("fragrant guaco") — _M. laevigata_, the more aromatic species.
- *Guaco-da-mata* ("forest guaco") — Atlantic-Forest wild form.
- *Erva-de-cobra* ("snake herb") — preserving the indigenous-anti-snakebite register.
- *Erva-de-serpente* — alternative snake-herb vernacular.
- *Coração-de-jesus* ("Jesus's heart") — religious-iconographic vernacular (the heart-shape of the leaves of some Mikania species).
- *Cipó-catinga* — Tupi-derived ("smelly vine") for the aromatic register.

*Spanish (broader South-American):* *guaco* (Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Argentina) — universal across Spanish-speaking South America. *Bejuco de guaco* ("guaco vine") in Caribbean and Central American Spanish.

*Indigenous (Cumanagoto, Carib, Chibcha-family languages):* *guaco / huaco / wako* — the original indigenous-South-American name with the snake-bite-curing register.

*Tupi-Guarani:* *cipó-catinga*, *guaco-da-mata* — regional Tupi-Portuguese hybrid namings.

*Sanskrit / Hindi:* No canonical entry — the species is exclusively American. No Mikania species enters classical Ayurvedic pharmacology.

*Chinese:* No canonical Chinese entry. _Mikania micrantha_ has reached Asian flora as an invasive species but is not in Chinese pharmacopoeial use.

*Arabic / Persian:* No canonical Tibb entry — exclusively American.

== Classical Indigenous / Brazilian / South-American tradition

_This is the substantive tradition for guaco. The substance is exclusively South-American and has reached Brazilian phytopharmacy through the_ unique trajectory _of indigenous → caboclo → folk-medical → industrial-commercial pharmacy, with no parallel reception in the Eurasian canonical traditions. The_ Brazilian Pharmacopoeia (Farmacopeia Brasileira) _includes guaco as one of the most-extensively-monographed Brazilian native medicinals, and the_ xarope de guaco _is among the most-consumed over-the-counter respiratory products in Brazil._

*Indigenous (Cumanagoto, Carib, Chibcha-language family) tradition:* the *original guaco use* — *anti-snake-bite* and *anti-insect-bite* preparations; topical paste on the wound; oral decoction; the substance recognized as the *canonical American snake-bite-curing plant* across multiple pre-Columbian indigenous traditions. The famous *Humboldt and Bonpland* recorded the indigenous Venezuelan guaco-anti-snakebite use during their 1799-1804 South-American expedition, and brought the substance to European-scientific attention; Mikania guaco was named in their honor.

*Caboclo (rural-creole) Brazilian tradition:* the indigenous-anti-snakebite register continued, with the addition of the *substantial respiratory register* — *the canonical Brazilian folk antitussive and bronchitis remedy*. Taken as:
- *Chá de guaco* — infusion of leaves; the canonical Brazilian household cough preparation.
- *Xarope caseiro de guaco* — homemade guaco syrup; guaco juice + sugar + sometimes honey + sometimes alcohol; the canonical Brazilian-rural cough syrup, with documented continuous use across Brazilian rural-and-urban folk medicine.
- *Tintura de guaco* — alcoholic tincture; concentrated form.
- *Garrafada com guaco* — Brazilian compound folk-medical decoction in a glass bottle.

*Brazilian Pharmacopoeia and Anvisa registration:* *the canonical case of a Brazilian native medicinal reaching the modern industrial-pharmaceutical register*. The Anvisa-approved RENISUS includes guaco; the *xarope de guaco* commercial Brazilian products are sold in pharmacies and supermarkets nationwide as foundational over-the-counter respiratory medications; major Brazilian phytotherapeutic manufacturers (Hertz, Catarinense, Farmácia da Natureza, etc.) produce standardized guaco-coumarin syrups. The substance has migrated successfully from folk medicine to mainstream pharmacy in a way that few other Brazilian natives have managed.#footnote[Brazilian ethnobotanical and pharmacopoeial literature: Lorenzi & Matos, _Plantas Medicinais no Brasil_; Brazilian Pharmacopoeia (Farmacopeia Brasileira) monographs on _Mikania glomerata_ and _M. laevigata_; Anvisa RENISUS; the commercial xarope de guaco industry. Humboldt and Bonpland's record of indigenous guaco use: _Voyage aux régions équinoxiales du nouveau continent_ (1799-1804). The unique folk-to-pharmacy trajectory of guaco is documented in Brazilian phytopharmacy literature and is among the cleanest cases of indigenous-medicinal-to-modern-pharmaceutical continuity.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Not classical for_ Mikania glomerata _specifically. Reasoned cross-system placement only._

By reasoned placement on the antitussive-and-expectorant axis (anchored on the Brazilian folk register and the modern coumarin pharmacology, with closest parallel to the canonical Chinese 化痰止咳 antitussive-and-expectorant class such as 川貝母 _chuān bèi mǔ_, 杏仁 _xìng rén_, and 紫菀 _zǐ wǎn_): warm (溫) to neutral (平); pungent (辛), aromatic (芳香), slightly bitter (微苦); Lung (肺), Spleen (脾), Liver (肝). Reasoned actions: 化痰止咳平喘 (transforms phlegm, stops cough, calms wheezing — the principal action), 解表散寒 (releases the exterior and disperses cold), 解毒 (resolves toxicity — the indigenous anti-snakebite register), 消腫止痛 (reduces swelling and stops pain — topical). Cautions: heat-pattern bleeding; pregnancy; concurrent anticoagulants (the coumarin antiplatelet caveat). Reasoned, not citation.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Not classical for the Brazilian species. No Mikania species in the canonical Ayurvedic tradition. Reasoned cross-system placement only._

*Reasoned rasa:* Kaṭu (pungent), Tikta (bitter — secondary), Kaṣāya (astringent — modest).

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (light), Rūkṣa (dry — modest), Tīkṣṇa (sharp), Sūkṣma (subtle — the aromatic-coumarin volatile fraction).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Uṣṇa (warming) — modest to moderate.

*Reasoned vipāka:* Kaṭu (pungent).

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pacifies *Kapha* (strongly — the canonical Kapha-clearing pungent-warming-aromatic profile, central to the bronchitis-and-cold-pattern-cough indication); pacifies Vāta in modest dose (the warming-aromatic note); aggravates Pitta in excess.

*Reasoned karma:* _Kāsa-hara, śvāsa-hara_ (the canonical respiratory action, vindicated by Brazilian folk practice and modern coumarin pharmacology); _kapha-hara_; _vāta-anulomana_; _viṣaghna_ (anti-toxin — the indigenous anti-snakebite register, modestly supported by modern preclinical research); _vraṇa-ropaṇa_ (topical wound-healing); _krimi-ghna_ (anthelminthic, modest); _ucchvāsa-śuddhi_ (breath-clearing — the aromatic-coumarin register).

The substance does not appear in Caraka, Suśruta, or the major nighaṇṭus; the placement is reasoned cross-system.

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S, N, F, M)_

- *(F) Conditional / Brazilian folk:* Pitta excess; pregnancy at therapeutic dose; bleeding disorders; known Asteraceae allergy.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Coumarin anticoagulant interaction*: *the principal modern pharmacological caveat*. Coumarin (the natural plant coumarin, 1,2-benzopyrone, *not* the synthetic anticoagulant warfarin / dicumarol, which is a derivative of natural coumarin via spoiled-sweet-clover oxidation) has *modest anticoagulant activity at high doses*. *Concurrent guaco with warfarin, NOAC anticoagulants, aspirin, clopidogrel, and other anticoagulants / antiplatelets* increases bleeding risk; the interaction is documented in case reports and clinical-pharmacology literature. *Discontinue guaco 7-10 days before surgery*.
  - *Pregnancy*: *contraindicated at therapeutic dose* — the coumarin teratogenicity register (synthetic coumarins like warfarin are teratogens; the plant-coumarin register at typical guaco doses is less clearly teratogenic but the conservative position is avoidance).
  - *Hepatic disease*: caution; coumarins are hepatically metabolized.
  - *Bleeding disorders*: *contraindicated*.
  - *Pediatric*: short-term use only under qualified guidance; the modern Brazilian pediatric register accepts xarope de guaco at appropriate doses for short-term respiratory illness.
  - *Asteraceae cross-allergy*: documented; cross-reactivity with ragweed, chamomile, daisy.
  - *Diabetes (alcohol-syrup commercial preparations)*: the standard Brazilian xarope de guaco is sucrose-based; diabetic preparations should use sugar-free formulations.#footnote[Coumarin anticoagulant interaction: published case reports and Mills & Bone _Herb Contraindications and Drug Interactions_; the plant-coumarin vs. synthetic-coumarin pharmacology distinction is well documented but the clinical caveat of avoiding concurrent anticoagulant use is universal in modern Brazilian phytopharmacy guidance. Anvisa monograph for guaco includes the anticoagulant interaction warning.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Not classical in Tibb._ Mikania glomerata _is exclusively South-American and was unknown to the medieval Arabic-Persian medical world; no canonical attestation. No Arabic name. Modern Arabic / Persian / Urdu commercial usage is the transliteration_ ǧwākū / غواكو / گواکو. _Reasoned modern Unani placement on the respiratory-coumarin axis (entirely interpretive):_

*Mizāj* — by extension on the aromatic-respiratory-Asteraceae axis: *Hot and Dry in the 2nd degree* (paralleling thyme *zaʿtar* and the canonical Tibb respiratory-aromatic class).

*Af'āl* (reasoned): *munaffith / mukhrij al-balgham* (expectorant — the principal Brazilian indication); *muḥaffif al-suʿāl* (cough-relieving); *mufattiḥ* (deobstruent); *muqaṭṭiʿ li-l-balgham al-yābis* (cuts viscous phlegm); *muqawwī al-ṣadr* (chest tonic); *mukhrij li-l-sumūm* (anti-toxin — the indigenous anti-snakebite register); *muḥsin al-tanaffus* (improves respiration).

*Istemālāt* (reasoned): chronic bronchitis; productive cough; cold-pattern asthma; respiratory tract congestion; topical for snakebite / insect bite (the indigenous heritage register).

*Mawāne'*: hot-pattern conditions; pregnancy; bleeding disorders; concurrent anticoagulants; Asteraceae allergy.

The substance does not appear in Avicenna, Ibn al-Bayṭār, or Khazāyin al-Adwiya. See _tibb-al-arabi.typ_.

