No classical Ayurvedic formulas.

=== Western / Greco-Roman

No classical European tradition; exclusively American. *Modern Western post-19th-c. herbal medicine*: guaco entered European materia medica via Humboldt-Bonpland's 1799-1804 South-American expedition; brief 19th-c. European pharmacopoeial use (Brazilian and Portuguese pharmacopoeias adopted it; British and German pharmacopoeias gave brief monographs); the modern Western-herbal-medicine register treats guaco principally for respiratory and asthma applications, following the Brazilian phytotherapy framework.

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic) medicine

No classical Tibb tradition.

=== Brazilian / Indigenous / South-American — the substantive tradition

*The most-developed industrial-pharmacy register of any Brazilian native medicinal:*

- *Xarope de guaco (guaco syrup)* — *the canonical Brazilian commercial-pharmaceutical respiratory product*; sucrose-based syrup with guaco extract; available in nearly every Brazilian pharmacy and supermarket; consumed at industrial-scale volumes annually. Major Brazilian brands include Hertz, Catarinense, Farmácia da Natureza, Schraiber, Apsen, EMS, and others. Anvisa-registered with standardized coumarin content.
- *Chá de guaco (guaco tea)* — household infusion of 3-5 g dried leaves in 250 mL hot water; for cough, bronchitis, asthma, and respiratory weakness.
- *Tintura de guaco* — alcoholic tincture; 1-3 mL diluted; concentrated form.
- *Garrafada com guaco* — Brazilian compound folk-medical decoction; commonly combined with *assa-peixe* (see _assa-peixe.typ_), *eucalipto*, *romã* (pomegranate), and other Brazilian respiratory herbs.
- *Topical guaco preparations* for snakebite (the indigenous heritage register); the modern clinical evidence for snake-bite efficacy is *very limited* and the substance should *not* substitute for proper antivenom treatment in clinical envenomation. The folk-traditional register is historical-cultural rather than clinically-effective for serious snake envenomation.
- *Guaco-and-honey combination*: a foundational Brazilian household cough remedy.#footnote[Brazilian xarope de guaco commercial register: Anvisa-registered phytotherapeutic products; the substance is among the most-consumed over-the-counter respiratory products in Brazil. The folk-traditional snakebite use should not substitute for proper antivenom in clinical practice; the cultural-historical register is documented but the clinical efficacy for serious envenomation is limited.]

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_M. glomerata_ is principally a *subtropical-humid Brazilian Atlantic Forest* species; native to Southeast and Southern Brazilian Atlantic Forest, with cultivated populations throughout Brazil. _M. laevigata_ has similar distribution. The genus _Mikania_ is pantropical and has Asian-and-African native species. See _climates.typ_.

*Subtropical-humid Atlantic Forest (the canonical region for_ M. glomerata _and_ M. laevigata_).* The climate carries hot wet summers and mild dry winters, with substantial atmospheric humidity year-round; the guaco vine climbs on Atlantic Forest understory trees and produces its small white flowers in late summer / autumn. The plant's coumarin chemistry is partly a *plant-defensive response* to humid-tropical fungal-and-insect pressure — coumarins have natural antimicrobial activity that gives the plant its competitive niche, and the same coumarin chemistry that gives guaco its medicinal-respiratory action is its biological-survival pharmacology. In Ayurvedic _ṛtucaryā_ terms the climate carries _grīṣma_-_varṣā_ Pitta-Kapha load with mild winter Vāta; in 中醫 terms the climate is 暑濕 (summer-heat-damp) on the Spleen-Heart and 寒 (winter cold) on the Lung; in Tibb terms the constitutional load is Hot-Moist (balgham) in summer and Cold-Dry in winter. *The traditional response and the guaco register*: the warming-aromatic-pungent-coumarin character is constitutionally apt for the *cold-pattern winter bronchitis and damp-Kapha pulmonary stagnation* that this climate generates; the *seasonal harvest* of guaco leaves in autumn and the *xarope production* in winter aligns with the canonical respiratory-illness season. The pairing with *assa-peixe* (more bitter-respiratory) and *eucalipto* (more aromatic-respiratory) covers the full respiratory-pharmacology spectrum.

*Andean and Caribbean (warm-and-cool-tropical):* the related species _M. cordifolia_, _M. guaco_, and others extend the genus across the tropical Americas. The indigenous anti-snakebite register is most-developed in the *Caribbean and Northern Andean (Venezuelan, Colombian)* indigenous traditions — coinciding with the regional snake-fauna density (the genus _Bothrops_, the canonical Latin-American venomous snake genus, is densest in these regions).

*Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26).* Guaco's *xarope de guaco* commercial register reaches the global Brazilian-diaspora communities and increasingly the broader Latin-American-diaspora and international-herbal-medicine commerce. *The respiratory indication is essentially climate-neutral* — chronic bronchitis and asthma occur in every climate, and the coumarin mechanism is climate-independent. The constitutional logic favors cool-season / winter / damp-Kapha-pattern use; the substance is one of the cleaner cases of a folk-medicinal whose mechanism-of-action transcends climate. The *industrial-scale Brazilian xarope de guaco production* makes the substance globally accessible at affordable cost.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:*
- *Coumarin (1,2-benzopyrone)* — *the principal active and the modern Brazilian Pharmacopoeia marker compound*; ~0.3-1.5% of dried leaf, varying by species and cultivar. *_M. laevigata_ typically has higher coumarin than _M. glomerata_*; the species substitution in commercial supply has been justified partly on coumarin-content grounds. *Important pharmacology note*: the natural coumarin in guaco is *distinct from the synthetic anticoagulant coumarin derivatives* (warfarin, dicumarol — these are products of spoiled-sweet-clover hay oxidation, in which natural coumarin is converted to dicumarol with substantially-enhanced anticoagulant activity). Natural coumarin has *mild* anticoagulant activity and substantial *antitussive, expectorant, anti-inflammatory, and antimicrobial* activity.
- *Diterpenes* — kaurenoic acid, grandiflorenic acid, ent-kaurenoic acid derivatives; the characteristic Mikania-genus diterpene class.
- *Sesquiterpene lactones* — modest content (less than _Vernonia_ / _Vernonanthura_ — see _assa-peixe.typ_).
- *Flavonoids* — quercetin, kaempferol glycosides.
- *Tannins* — modest.
- *Essential oil (0.1-0.3%)* — caryophyllene, germacrene, sesquiterpenes; less prominent than the coumarin-and-diterpene fraction in pharmacological terms.
- *Stigmasterol, β-sitosterol*.#footnote[Phytochemistry: published Brazilian phytochemistry surveys on _Mikania glomerata_ and _M. laevigata_ in _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_, _Phytochemistry_, _Revista Brasileira de Farmacognosia_; the natural-coumarin vs. synthetic-warfarin pharmacology distinction is well documented; Brazilian Pharmacopoeia monographs include coumarin as the marker compound.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Antitussive*: documented preclinical activity in cough-suppression models; the modern correlate of the canonical Brazilian respiratory register. Clinical evidence is modest-to-moderate; the substance is among the better-studied Brazilian native medicinals at the respiratory pharmacology level.
- *Expectorant*: documented mucociliary clearance enhancement in animal models; the modern correlate of the classical productive-cough indication.
- *Bronchodilator*: documented in preclinical models; the coumarin-and-diterpene fractions contribute; the modern correlate of the asthma indication.
- *Anti-inflammatory*: preclinical broad-spectrum activity, coumarin-and-flavonoid mediated.
- *Antimicrobial*: in vitro broad-spectrum activity; the modern correlate of the indigenous wound-and-snakebite topical register.
- *Anti-allergic*: modest preclinical evidence.
- *Anti-coagulant (mild)*: the documented clinical-pharmacology caveat (above). The natural-plant-coumarin anticoagulant activity is *modest* compared to synthetic warfarin but is *clinically relevant* in concurrent anticoagulant use.
- *Snake-venom inhibition (preclinical)*: documented activity against certain _Bothrops_ venoms in preclinical models, partly justifying the indigenous heritage register; *but the clinical efficacy is insufficient* to substitute for proper antivenom treatment in serious envenomation.
- *Cross-system convergence*: the indigenous-South-American anti-snakebite + Brazilian-folk respiratory + reasoned cross-system anti-tussive placement + modern coumarin pharmacology converges cleanly on the respiratory register; the *folk-to-industrial-pharmacy transition* makes guaco one of the most-studied Brazilian native medicinals at the clinical-pharmacology level.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Xarope de guaco (commercial)*: per Anvisa-registered product labeling; typically 5-15 mL up to 3 times daily for short-term respiratory illness.
- *Dried leaves in infusion*: 3-5 g per cup, 2-3 cups daily for short-term respiratory complaint.
- *Tincture (1:5 in 40% ethanol)*: 1-3 mL up to 3 times daily.
- *Risk groups (absolute or relative contraindications)*:
  - *Pregnancy*: *contraindicated at therapeutic dose*.
  - *Concurrent anticoagulants / antiplatelets*: *contraindicated*.
  - *Pre-surgical* (within 7-10 days of scheduled surgery): *discontinue*.
  - *Bleeding disorders*: *contraindicated*.
  - *Hepatic disease*: caution.
  - *Asteraceae allergy*: avoid.
  - *Diabetes*: use sugar-free or alcohol-free formulations.
  - *Pediatric*: standard Brazilian pediatric register accepts xarope de guaco at appropriate doses for short-term use; chronic use should be supervised.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*

